  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe majority of the forecast area should remain dry through this \nevening ahead of a period of unsettled weather. Low pressure pushing \neast across Ontario into Quebec will lift a warm front across the \nforecast area today. After a brief window of cooler conditions, \nhighs will rise into the low/mid 80s for most and the upper 70s for \neastern zones. Scattered showers and thunderstorms will approach the \nregion from the west as the low drags a cold front into the eastern \nGreat Lakes tonight. There remains some potential for an isolated \nstrong to severe thunderstorm in Northwest Ohio where the SWODY1 \nMarginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) remains.Primary hazard will be\ndamaging wind gusts. However, confidence remains fairly low \ngiven the disjointed timing of best instability and the arrival \nof the cold front. In addition to an isolated strong storm, \nthere remains the potential for locally heavy rainfall within \nthese storms across Northwest Ohio. The WPC maintains their Day \n1 Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall across \nNorthwest Ohio tonight. Will continue to monitor the locally \nheavy rainfall potential as recent runs of the HREF and REFS \nhighlight stripe of 6-hour rainfall amounts between 2 and 3 \ninches in their localized probability-matched mean products. \n\nThe cold front will waver across the Ohio Valley Wednesday into \nThursday. Additional rounds of showers and thunderstorms will be \nlikely through late week. There remains uncertainty on if any storms \nwill reach strong to severe limits mid to late week given the \nplacement of the surface boundary. Despite this, a very warm and \nmoist airmass will be in place with PWATs in the 1.5-2.0 inch \nrange. Any storm will be capable of producing heavy rainfall, \nparticulary in training showers and thunderstorms. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure and dry conditions briefly return Friday into\nearly Saturday before unsettled weather returns. A series of \nshortwaves aloft will move overhead Saturday into early next \nweek. Daily shower and thunderstorm chances continue Saturday \nafternoon into Monday, though forecast confidence decreases \nMonday onward.",
